1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:01,510
The Fullerton Building:

2
00:00:01,590 --> 00:00:04,700
The Post Office With a
Lighthouse on the Roof

3
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:08,903
For about twenty years, a
revolving beacon of 540,000

4
00:00:08,983 --> 00:00:15,045
candelas swept the harbour from the
roof of Singapore's General Post Office,

5
00:00:15,125 --> 00:00:18,600
visible from ships some
29 kilometres away.

6
00:00:18,600 --> 00:00:23,133
Many people who walk past the
building today, now a luxury hotel,

7
00:00:23,213 --> 00:00:26,975
have never heard that it
ever guided anyone anywhere.

8
00:00:26,975 --> 00:00:31,084
The site was named for Robert
Fullerton, the first Governor

9
00:00:31,164 --> 00:00:35,060
of the Straits Settlements,
and began as Fort Fullerton,

10
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:39,400
built around 1829 to guard the
mouth of the Singapore River.

11
00:00:39,400 --> 00:00:44,459
By the 1880s the ground in front
of it had become Fullerton Square,

12
00:00:44,539 --> 00:00:48,450
dominated by the Post Office
and Exchange Building.

13
00:00:48,450 --> 00:00:52,618
In the middle of the square
stood the Tan Kim Seng fountain,

14
00:00:52,698 --> 00:00:55,600
whose strange afterlife is its own story.

15
00:00:55,600 --> 00:00:58,886
By the 1920s the General
Post Office was still

16
00:00:58,966 --> 00:01:02,105
working from older
premises on Collyer Quay,

17
00:01:02,185 --> 00:01:06,575
and the government had bigger
plans for the waterfront site.

18
00:01:06,575 --> 00:01:09,338
The buildings then on
it, among them the old

19
00:01:09,418 --> 00:01:11,793
Singapore Club and government offices,

20
00:01:11,873 --> 00:01:15,750
were cleared to make way for
something on a different scale.

21
00:01:15,750 --> 00:01:19,362
The idea dated from before
the First World War,

22
00:01:19,442 --> 00:01:23,918
but active work only began in
1920, when Major Percy Keys,

23
00:01:23,998 --> 00:01:29,025
of the Shanghai firm Keys &
Dowdeswell, was appointed architect.

24
00:01:29,025 --> 00:01:33,575
Postwar financial stringency
stalled the project for years.

25
00:01:33,575 --> 00:01:38,514
Tenders were not called until
November 1923, and the contract

26
00:01:38,594 --> 00:01:43,122
went to Perry & Co. (Overseas)
Limited in February 1924,

27
00:01:43,202 --> 00:01:47,975
on a four-year schedule that
the builders very nearly met.

28
00:01:47,975 --> 00:01:52,450
According to NLB's BiblioAsia,
the site sat below tide level,

29
00:01:52,530 --> 00:01:55,735
so the foundations were
poured as a floating

30
00:01:55,815 --> 00:01:59,250
concrete raft with pumps
running continuously.

31
00:01:59,250 --> 00:02:03,410
Sir Hugh Clifford, the Governor,
opened it at ten o'clock

32
00:02:03,490 --> 00:02:06,386
on the morning of the 27th of June 1928,

33
00:02:06,466 --> 00:02:11,525
beneath the main portico, and used
the occasion to give it its name.

34
00:02:11,525 --> 00:02:16,100
The original tender had come
in just under $4.1 million,

35
00:02:16,180 --> 00:02:20,920
and the total vote, including
fittings, was $4.75 million,

36
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:24,325
which he expected would not be exceeded.

37
00:02:24,325 --> 00:02:26,682
Clifford called it the most imposing

38
00:02:26,762 --> 00:02:29,200
building in the Straits Settlements.

39
00:02:29,200 --> 00:02:33,062
It stood 120 feet tall behind
a colossal Doric colonnade,

40
00:02:33,142 --> 00:02:36,936
and it had been designed to
house far more than letters:

41
00:02:37,016 --> 00:02:40,394
the Post Office, ten other
government departments,

42
00:02:40,474 --> 00:02:43,725
the Singapore Club and
the Chamber of Commerce.

43
00:02:43,725 --> 00:02:48,000
The Straits Times described
the interior the same day.

44
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,050
The public counter was 300 feet
long, "probably the longest

45
00:02:52,130 --> 00:02:55,900
in the world," and a letter
dropped in the postbox fell

46
00:02:55,980 --> 00:02:59,750
to the sub-ground floor, rode
a 200-foot conveyor along

47
00:02:59,830 --> 00:03:03,750
the basement and was shot
onto a sorting table upstairs.

48
00:03:03,750 --> 00:03:07,416
Small windowed galleries
ran along the sorting-room

49
00:03:07,496 --> 00:03:11,602
ceiling so that supervisors
could watch the staff unseen,

50
00:03:11,682 --> 00:03:15,575
a design copied from the
big post offices in Britain.

51
00:03:15,575 --> 00:03:18,932
The most unusual feature
was a subway beneath

52
00:03:19,012 --> 00:03:22,525
Fullerton Road that led
to a Post Office pier.

53
00:03:22,525 --> 00:03:27,339
Launches serving the ships in the
harbour loaded and unloaded mail there,

54
00:03:27,419 --> 00:03:31,375
and the mail travelled up and
down by lift or spiral chute.

55
00:03:31,375 --> 00:03:34,489
The paper noted that
residents who remembered

56
00:03:34,569 --> 00:03:38,180
messengers carrying baskets
of mail across Fullerton

57
00:03:38,260 --> 00:03:41,525
Road in the old days
would be grateful for it.

58
00:03:41,525 --> 00:03:44,339
There was also a quirk in the arrangements

59
00:03:44,419 --> 00:03:46,900
that the Governor mentioned himself.

60
00:03:46,900 --> 00:03:50,858
Singapore had been given the
grandest post office in Malaya,

61
00:03:50,938 --> 00:03:54,290
yet the head office of the
Postal Department itself

62
00:03:54,370 --> 00:03:57,857
would stay in Kuala Lumpur,
because the telegraph and

63
00:03:57,937 --> 00:04:01,975
telephone networks it also ran
were administered from there.

64
00:04:01,975 --> 00:04:05,033
Having so many tenants under one roof made

65
00:04:05,113 --> 00:04:08,400
the building a small
town on the waterfront.

66
00:04:08,400 --> 00:04:12,941
In February 1942, in the final
days of the Battle of Singapore,

67
00:04:13,021 --> 00:04:17,268
it served as a makeshift
hospital, and Governor Sir Shenton

68
00:04:17,348 --> 00:04:21,675
Thomas sheltered in the Singapore
Club on its upper floors.

69
00:04:21,675 --> 00:04:24,686
After the surrender it became a base for

70
00:04:24,766 --> 00:04:27,625
the Japanese military administration.

71
00:04:27,625 --> 00:04:32,250
In peacetime, the building went
on doing what it was designed for.

72
00:04:32,250 --> 00:04:36,274
The Post Office, the Marine
Department and other government

73
00:04:36,354 --> 00:04:39,057
offices worked side by side for decades,

74
00:04:39,137 --> 00:04:43,450
and the Inland Revenue Department
was among the later tenants.

75
00:04:43,450 --> 00:04:46,232
On the 14th of December 1958,

76
00:04:46,312 --> 00:04:49,175
a beacon was lit on the roof.

77
00:04:49,175 --> 00:04:52,271
It was built by the
British firm Stone-Chance,

78
00:04:52,351 --> 00:04:57,517
stood about 48 metres above the sea,
and replaced the light at Fort Canning,

79
00:04:57,597 --> 00:05:00,417
whose 1903 tower had been steadily blocked

80
00:05:00,497 --> 00:05:04,225
by new high-rises and was
switched off the same month.

81
00:05:04,225 --> 00:05:07,674
The sources I found don't
record why a navigation

82
00:05:07,754 --> 00:05:10,195
light went on top of a post office,

83
00:05:10,275 --> 00:05:14,525
but the building was already
home to the Marine Department.

84
00:05:14,525 --> 00:05:18,250
The light did not last
as long as the building.

85
00:05:18,250 --> 00:05:22,152
Fort Canning's had been overtaken
by the growth of the city,

86
00:05:22,232 --> 00:05:25,869
and the Fullerton Light met
the same fate as towers went

87
00:05:25,949 --> 00:05:29,600
up and reclaimed land spread
around the old waterfront.

88
00:05:29,600 --> 00:05:34,079
Bedok Lighthouse, Singapore's
first unmanned lighthouse,

89
00:05:34,159 --> 00:05:36,603
began operating in August 1978,

90
00:05:36,683 --> 00:05:41,650
and the Fullerton Light was
decommissioned in the late 1970s.

91
00:05:41,650 --> 00:05:44,670
Sources differ on whether

92
00:05:44,750 --> 00:05:47,850
that was in 1978 or 1979.

93
00:05:47,850 --> 00:05:50,675
The lantern itself survived.

94
00:05:50,675 --> 00:05:54,920
It was moved to the Maritime
Museum in 1985, and later to

95
00:05:55,000 --> 00:05:59,853
HarbourFront and then to the
entrance of Mapletree Business City,

96
00:05:59,933 --> 00:06:03,575
where it has since been
refitted with LED lamps.

97
00:06:03,575 --> 00:06:07,413
The General Post Office
moved out in March 1996,

98
00:06:07,493 --> 00:06:11,575
and the building was
conserved the following year.

99
00:06:11,575 --> 00:06:15,399
Sino Land and Far East
Organization converted

100
00:06:15,479 --> 00:06:19,216
it into The Fullerton
Hotel, which opened on

101
00:06:19,296 --> 00:06:23,200
the 1st of January 2001
with about 400 rooms.

102
00:06:23,200 --> 00:06:26,999
In December 2015 the building was gazetted

103
00:06:27,079 --> 00:06:31,050
a national monument,
the 71st in Singapore.

104
00:06:31,050 --> 00:06:34,337
The Post Office counter,
the mail subway and the

105
00:06:34,417 --> 00:06:38,275
rooftop beacon are all gone,
but the colonnade remains.

106
00:06:38,275 --> 00:06:42,788
Why it matters today: Almost
everyone who sees the Fullerton

107
00:06:42,868 --> 00:06:46,925
knows it as a hotel, and
some know it as the old GPO.

108
00:06:46,925 --> 00:06:52,350
Far fewer know that it was built as the
whole waterfront's working headquarters,

109
00:06:52,430 --> 00:06:55,034
with a 300-foot counter, a mail subway,

110
00:06:55,114 --> 00:07:00,000
ships' launches at its door and, for
twenty years, a lighthouse on top.

111
00:07:00,000 --> 00:07:03,608
It is a reminder that a
building's best-known use

112
00:07:03,688 --> 00:07:07,300
is often only one of
several lives it has lived.
